WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:01.600
(polar bear whining)

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.100
- It's been four months

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.880
since your last bite.
(snow crunching)

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.440
You may fool yourself into
digging out some frozen kelp

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.880
but you know that dinner
actually sits comfortably

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.600
(seal purring)
200 miles away.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.920
It's so frustrating.
(polar bear groaning)

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.280
The last thing you need right now

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.640
is a huge male looking for trouble.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.080
(polar bear roaring)

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.500
(deafening winds)

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:40.980
At first glance,
(dramatic orchestra music)

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.440
the Arctic feels brutally devoid of life,

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.220
though a select group of animals

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.140
have figured out a way to thrive.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.460
(seal moaning)

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.740
(polar bears moaning)
But the real force here

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.580
works quietly underfoot.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.200
Every winter, sea ice
starts small and delicate.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:58.860
(ice crackling)
Then it grows

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.180
over millions of square miles.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.540
How is it possible that this freezer

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.680
holds the key to our entire planet?

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.740
(ice cracking)
Like a lung,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:08.880
sea ice breathes life

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.800
into the Arctic every year.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.160
In a cycle that impacts the
climate across the globe.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.180
This is the story from the
edge of the Canadian Arctic

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.200
where it all starts.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.840
My name is Bertie Gregory

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.360
and I'm a National Geographic
wildlife filmmaker.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.600
Everything is at stake.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.400
They're all waiting for the Big Freeze.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.320
(temperature clock ticking)

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.280
(waves crashing)
(mellow orchestra music)

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.840
Temperatures are starting

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.160
to drop in the Hudson Bay,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.520
but it needs to get a lot colder

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.200
for the salt water to
completely freeze over.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.600
Until then, the seals
are just too far away

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.520
and the hungry bears have
no choice but to wait.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.580
(deafening wind)
This guy is a big bruiser

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.180
and he's coming in, like
all the other polar bears,

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.180
they're coming to the water's edge,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.700
ready for the Big Freeze.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.880
But right now, the ice isn't quite ready.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.420
You can see him (chuckles)
kind of falling through it

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.380
and stumbling on his way.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.620
(snow crunching)

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.400
You know, you spend four
or five months on that,

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.020
starving, not eating anything.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.000
You know that as soon as that sea freezes,

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.060
that's your fast food buffet is ready.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.440
(sad orchestra music)

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:46.760
(winds roaring)

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.080
The Arctic hosts our planet's
most brutal snowstorms.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.040
(snow crunching)
But today, weather is

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.640
the last thing this bear
needs to worry about.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.520
(dramatic music)

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.880
He's moving on.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.600
This big male on the left approaching

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:08.380
the even bigger male on the right,

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.180
so we about to get some action.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.240
(snow crunching)
(polar bear growling)

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.840
(thrilling orchestra music)

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.520
(polar bears roaring)
(climatic orchestra music)

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.620
Oh, they're just battering each other.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.280
(polar bears grunting)

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.040
In case, we've added an unnecessary amount

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.820
of dramatic music.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.420
You be forgiven for
thinking that these two guys

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.360
are in some brutal fight to the death,

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.680
but actually this time of year,

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.920
males don't really have
anything to fight over.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.680
Here, there's no females in heat.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:04.740
There's no food.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.460
And instead, they've got
a bit of a bromance going.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.840
(polar bear whining)
And now, I mean,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.940
look at them after hammering each other,

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.580
having a little snooze together.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.820
Although they're solitary
most of the year,

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.760
when males finally meet up,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.440
they can be really playful.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.720
They often pair up with a sparring buddy

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.280
because in a few months,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.720
they'll be on the ice fighting

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.280
in deadly battles over breeding rights.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.600
(polar bear groaning)

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.040
(playful orchestra music)
But polar bears

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.040
are incredibly well adapted

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.200
to the challenges of these hardships.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.320
Their enormous 12-inch white
paws are like snowshoes,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.120
spreading their weight on thin ice.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.480
Their short, gripping
claws and soft footpads

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.320
also prevent them from slipping.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.360
The bear's narrow head and long neck

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.220
allows them to grab seals through

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.280
their breathing holes in the ice.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.840
And if you have to pull a
600-pound seal out of the water,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.760
then a massive set of canines

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:13.760
with setback premolars,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.920
allow you to bit down deep.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.360
But hands down, their key adaptation

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.860
is their incredible thermal insulation.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.500
(winds roaring)
If we switch

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.340
from a normal camera like this camera

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.060
and the camera you're
looking at me on now,

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.820
to a thermal camera,

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:31.980
you're now looking at me in heat.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.120
So, if I take my glove off,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.080
you see how my hands and
my face are bright orange.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.360
That means, I'm losing
heat to the environment

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.520
like there is no tomorrow.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.560
I'm so badly adapted to this place.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.280
Whereas, if you look at the bear,

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.900
he's that dark purple, almost black color.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.340
And that is because of
that thick, thick fur,

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.380
which obviously I don't have.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.340
My biggest challenge here
is keeping warm enough,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.120
whereas his is keeping cool enough.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:01.560
(wind howling)

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:02.900
(pensive orchestra music)
Hang on, Steve.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:04.180
You see that bear over there?

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.560
He's coming in pretty good.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:09.940
- Hey, buddy.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.280
You need to start thinking
twice about walking in.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.960
(snow crunching)

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.600
- There are very few animals

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.000
that will actually try and hunt people.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.400
And polar bears are one of those animals.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.120
- Hey, slow down.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:31.360
Slow down.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:35.540
Hey, we're not an easy meal.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.760
Hey, hey.
(rocks banging)

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.380
Slow down, hey!
(snow thrashing)

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:42.700
Slow down.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.240
I'm gonna hit you with the rock.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:55.840
Hey!

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:56.800
Close enough.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:58.880
No, no, no.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:06:59.900
No.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.960
(polar bear huffing)

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.020
Hey, sit down.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.420
(polar bear whining)
Sit.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:17.680
There you go.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.320
- Well, Steve told it to sit and it sat.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.100
(wheezes)

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.140
Unbelievable.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.280
- [Steven] Hey,

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:29.780
go play with your friend.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.560
- This bear will just have to wait

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.120
for the Big Freeze to get his dinner,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.560
along with the others,

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.880
all stalling for time along the coast.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:44.800
(polar bears whining)

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.340
(thoughtful music)

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.920
The most powerful force in the Arctic

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.680
starts small, really small.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:57.760
(ice crackling)

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:01.080
So fragile, that it could
break in your fingertips.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.480
It grows inch by inch,

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.360
almost when no one's looking.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.240
But once it starts,

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.640
there's no stopping.
(dramatic orchestra music)

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.680
The water hits 28.8 Fahrenheit

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.700
and the Big Freeze has finally arrived.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:30.240
(stern violin music)

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:42.780
And finally what was
thousands of miles of liquid

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.400
has turned into this
rock-solid ice pathway

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.320
across the ocean.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.920
(strict violin music)

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:53.200
Polar bears have depended on
The Big Freeze for millennia.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.180
The problem is that our warming climate

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.740
is delaying its arrival by
more than a day each year.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.960
That means, that this generation

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:05.040
has lost at least a month of hunting

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.680
that previous ones relied on.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:08.720
(seal purring)

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.320
We've got to turn this around

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.360
and save our planet's
most vital cooling system.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.120
(snow crunching)
(polar bear grunting)

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.560
And off he goes

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:23.760
Slowly but surely, our
bear starts his trek

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.520
out over the ocean,

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.840
for as long as the ice will let him.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:33.280
(snow crunching)
(dramatic orchestra music)

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:57.100
(sad piano music)

